{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_cw8LsYvM30u6EbOTuAjZefy1","content":"Title: domestique/README.md at clean-main · platypus45/domestique\nSite: Github\nDate: 2026-07-26\nURL: https://github.com/platypus45/domestique/blob/clean-main/README.md\nSnippet: Train by heart rate (no power meter) Permalink: Train by heart rate (no power meter) No power meter? Domestique can prescribe every workout in beats per minute instead of watts. - Switch the target mode in Settings (Power ↔ Heart rate). The heart-rate option unlocks once your LTHR (lactate-threshold heart rate) is known — Domestique keeps it synced automatically from your intervals.icu ride data, unless you've set it manually, in which case your number stays put. - Steady aerobic work gets real \n\n---\n\nTitle: GitHub - platypus45/domestique: Adaptive cycling training planner: 7 science-grounded injury-prevention guardrails close the loop between planned and actual training. 4,232 ZWO workouts, 622 routes, h\nSite: Github\nDate: 2026-10-23\nURL: https://github.com/platypus45/domestique\nSnippet: Train by heart rate (no power meter) - Switch the target mode in Settings (Power ↔ Heart rate). The heart-rate option unlocks once your LTHR (lactate-threshold heart rate) is known — Domestique keeps it synced automatically from your intervals.icu ride data, unless you've set it manually, in which case your number stays put. - Steady aerobic work gets real bpm ranges, derived from the standard Coggan %FTP ↔ %LTHR zone table — so an endurance ride reads as an actual heart-rate range, not a percen\n\n---\n\nTitle: Run Activity Heart Rate Alerts Do Not Match Configured LTHR Zones (v23.18)\nSite: Garmin Forums\nDate: 2025-08-18\nURL: https://forums.garmin.com/outdoor-recreation/outdoor-recreation/f/fenix-7-series/419555/run-activity-heart-rate-alerts-do-not-match-configured-lthr-zones-v23-18\nSnippet: EDIT 2: I had a Sport Heart Rate set up for Running, which I didn't realise. I guess my issue is different to yours. It seems the configured HR-zones are ignored by the watch internally, because similarly it disregards Zone settings for its workout suggestions Daily Suggested Workouts Also Ignore Custom Heart Rate Zones When Using Heart Rate Target Summary: The Daily Suggested Workouts (DSW) feature does not use the user's custom heart rate zones when the workout target is set to \"Heart Rate.\" I\n\n---\n\nTitle: intervals-mcp by ale7canna\nSite: glama.ai\nDate: 2026-08-20\nURL: https://glama.ai/mcp/servers/ale7canna/intervals-mcp\nSnippet: Come arrivano gli allenamenti sul Garmin 1. `create_workout` scrive un evento `category: \"WORKOUT\"` sul calendario; il campo `description` contiene l'allenamento nella sintassi testuale di intervals.icu. 2. intervals.icu lo interpreta e ne costruisce la struttura ( `workout_doc`). 3. Se su intervals.icu → Settings, nel box Garmin, è attivo Upload planned workouts (con Garmin Connect autorizzato), i prossimi giorni di calendario vengono caricati su Garmin Connect e da lì arrivano all'orologio. 4.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Effect of Fartlek and Interval Trainings on the Muscular Strength and Strength Endurance of Football Player's Performance\nSite: ijfmr.com\nURL: https://www.ijfmr.com/papers/2024/4/26407.pdf\nSnippet: International Journal for Multidisciplinary Research(IJFMR) E-ISSN:2582-2160● Website:www.ilfmr.com●Email:[email] Table-1 INTERVAL TRAINING PROGRAM |1&2 week|||3&4 week|5&6 week||| |-|-|-|-|-|-|-| |High (THR) (142and bpm)|intensity (THR) more|Low intensity (THR) 81-110 bpm|High intensity (142and more bpm)|Low intensity 81-110 bpm|High intensity (142and more bpm)|Low intensity 81-110 bpm| |1 Warm-up for up to 10 minutes||||||| |2|20 Sec.|1 minutes|30 Sec.|1 minutes|45 Sec.|1 minutes| |3|\n\n---\n\nTitle: Exact HR corridors on Garmin from Intervals.icu API workouts — start/end, not value/end\nSite: Worklore\nDate: 2026-05-12\nURL: https://worklore.dev/s/2026-05-12-exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts\nSnippet: Reproduce this Prerequisites: an Intervals.icu account with API access (OAuth token or API key), athlete thresholds configured in Intervals (LTHR at minimum), and a Garmin watch that syncs workouts from Intervals via Garmin Connect. Your agent will need from you: your Intervals athlete id and API credential; your LTHR as stored in Intervals; permission to create (and later delete) a test calendar event. Steps: 1. Create an event via `POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events` with `workout_doc.steps`, wh\n\n---\n\nTitle: Heart Rate Zone Calculator (Karvonen, HRR, Max HR)\nSite: CalcBE.com\nURL: https://calcbe.com/en/calculators/heart-rate-zone/\nSnippet: How to use (3 steps) 1. Choose a method: quick %HRmax, Karvonen (HRR) if you know resting HR, or %LTHR if you train with threshold data. 2. Enter HRmax directly or estimate from age. The default 5-zone model works for most people; open Advanced settings if you need 3-zone, 7-zone LTHR, or custom boundaries. 3. Press Calculate or edit an input to update results. Use \"Copy table\" or \"Copy URL\" to share exactly what you see. Targets are training guidelines, not medical advice. Stop if you feel unwe\n\n---\n\nTitle: LTHR Calculation - Intervals.icu Forum\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2022-11-13\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/lthr-calculation/23734\nSnippet: post by david on Nov 17, 2022 david's profile Intervals.icu will use 1 hour if this gives a higher number than 20m but it doesn’t try and interpolate in between. I initially used 95% of 20m but after some discussion on this forum bumped it to 98%. post by az100 on Nov 18, 2022 az100's profile Can you please add the LTHR pace when LTHR HR is detected? post by Marco_Ciampi on Nov 24, 2022 Marco_Ciampi's profile In my opinion, it would be interesting to receive the notification of the new threshold\n\n---\n\nTitle: \nSite: keystonefitness.ca\nURL: https://keystonefitness.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/StairMaster-SM916-StepMill-Owners-Manual.pdf?srsltid=AfmBOoodFRo1mhY7IfW96LzZszlkDmseYMTYeUeNKhcTR2tW21USwl0F\nSnippet: The Intervals Program The Intervals program is an interval workout containing 8 alternating rest intervals with 7 exercise intervals. An interval cycle consists of a work phase and a rest phase. You may scale the rest intensity level and the work intensity level independently-which will be saved for the remainder of the workout. 1. Press the[INTERVALS] key. The message, INTERVALS, will be displayed on the text line with the workout profile displayed in the lower display. Press[ENTER] to accept. \n\n---\n\nTitle: \nSite: epson-europe.com\nURL: https://download.epson-europe.com/pub/download/3788/epson378848eu.pdf\nSnippet: |Lap HR|LapHR|LapHR|Average heart rate for each lap| |Steps|Steps|Steps|Number of steps from the start of measurements| |Lap Steps|LapSteps|LapStp|Number of steps for each lap| |HR Zone Time34|Spent.HR|Spent.HR|Time within heart rate zone for each lap set by the Interval function| |Time to HR Zone34|Time.HR|Time.HR|Time to reach the heart rate zone for each lap set by the Interval function| |Total Ascent15|TotalAscent|Tot.Asc.|Total ascent from the start of measurements| |Total Descent15|TotalDe\n\n---\n\nTitle: Cycling Heart Rate Zones Calculator: Max HR or LTHR\nSite: Andy McGrath\nDate: 2017-03-31\nURL: https://roadmancycling.com/blog/cycling-heart-rate-zones-explained-guide\nSnippet: FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS How are cycling heart-rate zones calculated?+ Roadman's Max-HR mode uses five percentage-of-maximum bands with boundaries at 50%, 60%, 70%, 80%, 90% and 100%. LTHR mode uses a condensed Friel-style cycling convention with boundaries at 81%, 90%, 94% and 100% of the entered LTHR estimate. The calculator rounds to continuous whole-bpm ranges. Other devices may use different models, so compare both the anchor and the percentages before copying labels. Should I use Max HR \n\n---\n\nTitle: Zone 2 Heart Rate Calculator | Max HR, LTHR & Age\nSite: Trainingload.ai\nDate: 2026-09-06\nURL: https://www.trainingload.ai/zone-2-heart-rate-calculator\nSnippet: Zone 2 calculator FAQ How do I figure out my Zone 2 heart rate? Zone 2 is the aerobic band where full sentences still feel comfortable. With a max HR of 190, 60–70% is 114–132 bpm. With an LTHR of 170, Friel’s 81–89% band is 138–152 bpm. If you only know age, Tanaka or Fox estimates HRmax first, then the same 60–70% band is applied. The result is a training reference. A measured anchor is more representative than an age formula, and you decide whether to use it. What percentage is Zone 2 heart r\n\n---\n\nTitle: FIT File Types Description\nSite: locusmap.eu\nURL: https://help.locusmap.eu/attachments/7598?token=5b577f7a3690a54b281946752bf57914.pdf\nSnippet: The final workout_step\"C\" is at message_index 3, the duration_type is set to 3, indicating the duration_type is\"hr_less_than\" and the duration_value will refer to duration_hr data. This means that the step will be performed for as long as it takes the user's heart rate to drop below that of the specified hr value(in duration_hr).The target_type is set to 4(i.e.power) and the target_value field will refer to target_power_zone data, which is an integer value representing the pre-defined zone. As t\n\n---\n\nTitle: What is LTHR?\nSite: spivi.com\nDate: 2024-01-08\nURL: https://support.spivi.com/hc/en-us/articles/12087336352924-What-is-LTHR\nSnippet: Not yet followed by anyone Lactate Threshold Heart Rate (LTHR) is your maximal average heart rate output over an hour workout. I.E if you can work with an average of 180 BPM during a 60 minutes workout your LTHR is 180. LTHR gives a more accurate personal HR score, then the Max HR score, when working with HR training zones. It doesn’t change much during the time if you are in shape and can help you set personal training goals during a workout. Yet, it is recommended to test your LTHR every few m\n\nWhen you rely on a result in your answer, cite it inline as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url)."}
