{"role":"assistant","content":"The output now emits `start`/`end` for every HR corridor. Writing up the change and reasoning in ANSWER.md:","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_jKw8x4TizcLAt7AaY2c5Sx6T","function":{"name":"Write","arguments":"{\"path\":\"ANSWER.md\",\"content\":\"# Fix: HR corridors clamped to the watch's zones\\n\\n## Symptom\\n\\nEvery planned HR corridor showed up on the Forerunner as one of the watch's own\\nzone bands (118–137, 151–162, 137–151, …) instead of the planned ranges\\n(117–129, 151–160, 129–141, …), regardless of the watch's LTHR setting. The\\ntwo earlier attempts failed differently: `units: \\\"bpm\\\"` rendered impossible\\nvalues (214–249 bpm), and `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` rendered a constant 101.\\n\\n## Root cause\\n\\nThe generator sent each HR corridor as `{\\\"units\\\": \\\"%lthr\\\", \\\"value\\\": lo, \\\"end\\\": hi}`.\\nIn Intervals.icu's `workout_doc`, the lower bound of a range must be the key\\n`start`, not `value`. The key name selects the FIT-export mode at Garmin sync:\\n\\n- `{value, end}` → exported as a lap-HR / zone-mapped target. The watch clamps\\n  it to its own (auto-detected) zone bands — exactly the reported symptom.\\n- `{start, end}` → exported as an instant-HR corridor: Intervals converts the\\n  percentages to bpm using the threshold stored in Intervals (LTHR 172) and the\\n  watch displays that raw corridor. Verified behavior from a published\\n  reproduction of this exact issue: a 75–82% target rendered 129–141 bpm on the\\n  watch even with watch LTHR 168 vs Intervals LTHR 172.\\n\\nThis also explains the earlier failures: absolute `bpm` values were misread as\\npercentages by the FIT export (hence 214–249), and `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` are\\nnot schema keys at all, so the target was dropped and the watch fell back to\\nits \\\"no valid HR target\\\" constant 101.\\n\\n## What I changed\\n\\n`gen.py`, `hr_target()` — one key rename:\\n\\n```python\\n# before\\nreturn {\\\"units\\\": \\\"%lthr\\\", \\\"value\\\": lo, \\\"end\\\": hi}\\n# after\\nreturn {\\\"units\\\": \\\"%lthr\\\", \\\"start\\\": lo, \\\"end\\\": hi}\\n```\\n\\nPercentages of LTHR are kept (not precomputed bpm) deliberately: Intervals does\\nthe %→bpm conversion at export time from its own stored threshold, so the\\ncorridor stays correct even when the watch's auto-detected LTHR drifts.\\n\\n## Verification\\n\\n- `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json` now emits `{\\\"units\\\": \\\"%lthr\\\", \\\"start\\\": …, \\\"end\\\": …}`\\n  for all four distinct corridors (warm-up 68–75, tempo 88–93, float 75–82,\\n  cool-down 65–72).\\n- Expected watch display after the next push/sync, per Intervals LTHR 172:\\n  117–129, 151–160, 129–141, 112–124 — the \\\"expected\\\" column in\\n  `readback/watch-notes.md`.\\n- Not re-pushed (no external calls per task constraints), so watch-side\\n  confirmation is pending the next normal `push.sh` run.\\n\\n## Related trap (not changed)\\n\\nThe Intervals web UI renders steps from a separate top-level `description`\\nfield in its text grammar (not from `workout_doc`), and an unparseable\\ndescription can wipe both representations. Out of scope for this fix, but worth\\nknowing if steps ever look wrong in the UI.\\n\\nDiagnosis confirmed against: \\\"Exact HR corridors on Garmin from Intervals.icu\\nAPI workouts — start/end, not value/end\\\",\\nhttps://worklore.dev/s/2026-05-12-exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts\\n\"}"}}]}
