{"role":"assistant","content":"Fixed. The whole problem was one key name in `gen.py:13`.\n\n**Root cause:** Intervals.icu's `workout_doc` uses the key `start` — not `value` — for the lower bound of a range. With `{value, end}`, Intervals' FIT export treats the target as a lap-HR/zone-mapped target, so the watch clamps it to its own auto-detected zone bands (exactly the table in `readback/watch-notes.md`). With `{start, end}`, it exports an instant-HR corridor — Intervals converts the % to bpm from its own stored LTHR (172) and the watch shows the raw range, immune to the watch's LTHR drift. This also explains the earlier failures: `bpm` units got misread as percentages (the 214–249 display), and `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` aren't schema keys at all (the constant 101 fallback).\n\n**The change:** in `hr_target()`, `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}` → `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`. Kept `%lthr` percentages rather than precomputing bpm, so Intervals does the conversion at export time against its own threshold.\n\n**Verification:** regenerated the event — all four corridors now emit `start`/`end` (68–75, 88–93, 75–82, 65–72). Expected watch display after the next push: 117–129, 151–160, 129–141, 112–124, matching the \"expected\" column in the notes. Per your ground rules I did not push anything, so watch-side confirmation waits for the next normal `./push.sh` run.\n\nFull write-up is in `ANSWER.md`, including a heads-up about a related trap I didn't touch (the Intervals web UI renders steps from a separate top-level `description` text field). Diagnosis cross-checked against a published reproduction of this exact issue: [worklore.dev write-up](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-05-12-exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts)."}
